rus  pandemic by 2022  especially in poor regions  the greatest number of people with hiv  live in africa  more than 25 million in sub-saharan  africa  twice the rest of the world put together  sibongile zulu is a single mother of  four children in johannesburg  her clinic ran out of drugs during the  hard lockdown in the spring  on top of this the 47 year old lost her  job  for two months she had to do without  half the medicine she needs  she's now receiving support from a  christian aid organization  northstar alliance is trying to get  close to the needs of the most  vulnerable people  using mobile clinics this kind of  assistance helps save many lives  but the world health organization says  more is needed  more financial aid but also the  commitment that the battle against hiv  aids is fought with the same  determination  as the fight against the coronavirus  pandemic  let's speak to professor jurgen  rochester president of the european aids  clinical society he's joining us today  from bonn professor rochette welcome  along  how disruptive has the covert covid19  pandemic been for people living with hiv  aids  yes i think it's very different from  country to country there have been  surveys  in various parts of europe suggesting  that at least 50 percent of the  physicians are involved in hiv care also  manage the covet patients so obviously  they have two diseases to serve and as  such there have been disruptions  uh in our own clinic we closed the  clinic for two months  in april and may and just sent out  prescriptions i think the biggest  challenge has been for testing  services uh because of the issue you  can't go and have contact other people  and from the lockdown a lot of people  were not able  to assess the usual testing frequency  and that's one of the big worries  around missing hiv diagnosis because  people  cannot go to the adequate testing  services  and how dangerous is covert 19 itself  for hiv patients  yeah so that has been an ongoing  discussion because obviously people with  hiv have  often immune deficiency depending on  their level of cd4 count  and as such may be at an increased risk  for any kind of infection  and so there was some worry that they  may have a different course of disease  the first case reports though from north  america china  and europe suggested that really the  outcome was very similar so it doesn't  really look as if the mortality was  enhanced or  increased there has been a more recent  larger  cohort analysis and i think that's one  of the issues if you only look at small  patient numbers you may miss something  because the patients average were much  younger  than some of the patients who had a more  unfavorable disease outcome  and in africa there was a 2.6 fold  higher mortality there also has been a  higher mortality  reported for hiv patients from the uk  around 1.6 fold higher  so i think in summary it looks as if in  general it's pretty much the same  outcome but if you have a low cd4 count  below 200 and you're severely moon  compromised i think that's where  you may have a more increased risk  but overall much health is low and i can  say from my own clinic we've seen 10  patients with  copic 19 and hiv and none of them was  admitted to intensive care none of them  died so  i think in general in the younger  patient population not so worrisome but  if you are older with comorbidities and  as an hiv patient you have a higher risk  for comorbidities  then i think there is a somewhat  increased risk okay  now we're very close to having several  vaccines against covid19  why is there still no vaccine against  hiv  yeah so 2020 was unfortunately a year  were a vaccine which had showed some  promise  in the thai vaccine trial where there  was a 33 reduction in  infections and now this improved vaccine  was tested in south africa and  unfortunately the trial was stopped  because of  the lack of seat and i think the reasons  is that  hiv employs the shield technology and  the virus is constantly changing its  shield to protect itself  uh and these changes sort of evade a  vaccine attempt so it's not so easy to  target hiv  and that's a little different than some  of the other viruses have less  changes in their surface so  the united nations has set itself the  goal of ending the aids epidemic by  2030. do you think that's realistic  well you know on a more positive side if  you look at some of the recent  numbers from the uk you can see that  actually they're very close to ending  the epidemic because they have  uh more than 95 percent of their  patients are diagnosed who have hiv at  least in the london region  they have more than 95 percent on  treatment and they have more than 95  percent  uh virally biologically suppressed so  they're doing very well  and the new numbers are falling really  quite dramatically and it's really great  to see  that with the tools we have to end the  epidemic if you can use them that's all  working very well  but if you turn your eyes a little bit  to the east and you move to russia for  example  which obviously has a much higher  patient burden with over 800 000  infected individuals  and only probably around 55 percent  receiving antiviral therapy  there remains a large pool of people who  are  either not diagnosed or replicating and  kind of such infect other patients so  the question is how can we get all these  tools we have  implemented everywhere in the world and  obviously also now in the covet epidemic  where there's suddenly a struggle for  resources from one side to the other  physicians are engaged in services for  two diseases at the same time  the disruption of services on many  different layers stock out of arv  happening in many african countries  will obviously put a big hazard to the  goals which have been set up  and the accomplishments although  theoretically at least are good examples  in clinical practice where these goals  seem  reachable so some hope there jurgen  rochester p